        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36569722?u=919b278a71956b9b30fea93a71dd49a50e81b168&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><p dir=\"auto\">Both clone() pages only mention a body that has already been used. Acquiring a body reader also makes cloning throw TypeError, even before the first read and while bodyUsed remains false.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Document the locked-body case and explain the getReader() trigger on both Request and Response. This follows Fetch's clone algorithms and its definition of an unusable body:<br>\n<a href=\"https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-request-clone\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-request-clone</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-response-clone\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-response-clone</a></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Checked in Node 24.22.0 for both interfaces: an unread locked body throws, releasing the unread reader lock allows cloning, and a consumed body still throws. Targeted Prettier and git diff --check pass. No browser or full content build run.</p>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46567",
            "title": "Explain locked bodies in Request and Response clone()",
            "date_modified": "2026-11-17T14:57:06.000Z",
            "date_published": "2026-11-17T14:52:53.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "ragib-mrb",
                "url": "https://github.com/ragib-mrb"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1795135?u=8b4759f2d0964426ac6f2aeacf061ad1ee0e24db&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><p dir=\"auto\">type() is supported in all 3 browsers as part of attr().</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">calc-mix(), random-item() are in Safari 27.3.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">integrity() is supported by Chrome and WebKit trunk</p>",
